.Dd November 10, 2026
.Dt USTAT 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm ustat
.Nd get the free space of a mounted file system by its device
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In ustat.h
.Ft int
.Fn ustat "dev_t dev" "struct ustat *ubuf"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn ustat
reports in
.Fa ubuf
the free blocks and free inodes of the mounted file system whose device
number is
.Fa dev ,
in the encoding
.Xr stat 2
gives
.Va st_dev :
the device node a volume was mounted from, or the number of its own
that a mount on no device has.
A bind mount shows a file system that is already mounted, and is not
one of its own.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
struct ustat {
    daddr_t f_tfree;    /* free blocks */
    ino_t   f_tinode;   /* free inodes */
    char    f_fname[6]; /* zero */
    char    f_fpack[6]; /* zero */
};
.Ed
.Pp
The counts are those of
.Xr statfs 2 :
.Va f_tfree
is its
.Va f_bfree ,
in its unit of
.Va f_bsize ,
and
.Va f_tinode
its
.Va f_ffree .
A file system that keeps no count of them, as
.Pa /proc ,
.Pa /dev
and
.Pa /sys
keep none, gives zero.
.Va f_tfree
is an int, and a count past it is cut to its low 32 bits, as Linux cuts
it.
.Ss System call numbers
The call is 62 on i386 and 136 on x86-64.
An i386 program's
.Vt struct ustat
is 20 bytes, with a 32-bit
.Va f_tinode ;
an x86-64 program's is 32, with a 64-bit one.
The x86-64 kernel gives a program that enters it by
.Ql int $0x80
the i386 layout, with both counts cut to 32 bits, as
.Xr ia32 7
describes.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Rv -std ustat
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/oldfsprobe.c
calls
.Fn ustat
with the
.Va st_dev
of a tmpfs directory and compares the counts with those
.Xr statfs 2
gives, and checks that a device number no mount has is
.Er EINVAL .
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Bq Er EINVAL
No mounted file system has the device number
.Fa dev .
.It Bq Er EFAULT
.Fa ubuf
is outside the caller's writable memory.
.El
.Pp
The error of the file system's own count, such as
.Er EIO
from a disk, is returned as it comes.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr stat 2 ,
.Xr statfs 2 ,
.Xr sysfs 2
.Sh STANDARDS
The call is Linux's, which still builds it, and its rules are those of
Linux 6.12.
musl declares it in
.In sys/ustat.h
only for some architectures; a program calls it through
.Xr syscall 2 .
The root of the boot image, whose
.Va st_dev
is 0, answers as any other mount does; Linux gives no file system that
number.
